The write was confirmed by both copies of shard 2 (`total 2, successful 2`). `_primary_term` is 2 because shard 2's primary was on os1 when the last Try-it stopped it, and its replica was promoted. The search found nothing until the refresh, while the `GET` found the document at once. The refresh went to all six copies. Shard 2 now has a second segment, `_1`, holding the one new document, on the primary and on the replica, because each copy indexes it. `committed false` means the segment isn't in a Lucene commit yet, so the translog is what keeps it safe. The plain search asked 3 shard copies, one per shard, and ranked M-0045 first. In shard 0, "bikes" is in one document of six, so it counts as rare there, while in shard 2 "northwind" is in three documents of five and counts for little. With numbers from every shard (`dfs_query_then_fetch`), M-0042, which has both words, comes first again, as on the single shard. Your primary term depends on which node you stopped in the last Try-it.

