The 512 MB heap keeps compressed object pointers, as any heap up to about 31 GB would. os1 listens on a real network address, so it enforces the startup checks (`bootstrap checks` in the log), and Docker Desktop's VM already has `vm.max_map_count` at the 262,144 they require. You get the two log lines once per start of os1, so twice if you stopped os1 in the second Try-it. The index created with no settings got 1 primary and 1 replica, and the defaults are the numbers this lesson used: a refresh every second, at most 10,000 for `from + size`, and one minute before a lost replica is rebuilt. The cluster allows 1,000 shard copies per node, stops placing shards at 85% disk, moves shards away at 90%, blocks writes at 95%, and copies recoveries at 41,943,040 bytes a second, which is 40 MB. `down -v` then deletes the cluster and its data.
